The starter splits the line for you. A `std::istringstream` (from `<sstream>`, covered properly in lesson 10.05) wraps a string so you can read from it with `>>` exactly as you read from `std::cin`: one word at a time, skipping spaces, until the text runs out.

```cpp
std::istringstream words{ "3 4 +" };
std::string token{};
while (words >> token) { std::cout << '[' << token << ']'; }   // [3][4][+]
```
